# Quiet-by-Design at MWAA — From the EM Director's Chair

**Author lens:** 25-year airport EM director at a top-tier US hub. Not a binder author. The person the CEO, chief of police, TSA FSD, and airline duty managers turn to in the first 30 minutes of an event, when nobody has caught up yet.

**Thesis reframed for this chair:** A quiet-by-design terminal standard is neither a threat nor a favor to emergency management. It is a *precondition* for effective emergency communication — but only if MWAA writes the standard so that the same acoustic envelope that makes normal operations calmer also makes emergency messages *intelligible*, and only if the standard hard-wires the redundant visual, digital, WEA, and staffed channels that must carry the message when the PA cannot. Written well, quiet-by-design strengthens the EM program. Written as a branding exercise, it erodes it.

---

## 1. Key findings (things a peer EM director would recognize as true)

1. **The loudest terminals are usually the least intelligible.** Speech Transmission Index (STI) collapses as background noise rises and reverberation lengthens. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 requires an STI of at least 0.45 (or CIS ≥ 0.65) at every occupant location in every acoustically distinguishable space where voice notification is required. Airports with untreated ceilings, hard floors, and unzoned overhead PA routinely fail. A quiet-by-design floor is therefore the fastest path to *compliant emergency intelligibility* — provided intelligibility is what we specify, not decibels.
2. **The failure mode in a real activation is not "too many announcements." It is "nobody heard the announcement that mattered."** FLL 2017 (Broward County AAR): the first hour saw uncontrolled self-evacuation, tarmac invasion by fleeing passengers, and an hour-and-a-half of secondary false-shooter panic. The airport's own PA was not the primary information channel for most passengers; rumor, personal cellphones, and social media were. Silence did not cause that. Uncoordinated multi-channel communication did.
3. **Quiet-by-design cuts both ways in an active EOC.** ATL December 2017: an 11-hour underground switchgear fire took down power to concourses, PA, jetways, elevators, escalators, and the FIDS network at once. When the primary PA and displays fail, an airport that has *engineered* redundant staffed information — trained ambassadors, battery-backed portable PA, mobile push, printed signage caches, and an EOC-controlled emergency override on any working zone — is the airport that keeps 30,000 stranded passengers from becoming a crowd-crush incident. An airport that has designed out the announcement muscle without building the substitutes has removed a life-safety layer.
4. **Announcement governance is an EOC function, not a customer-experience function.** Most airports treat PA scripts and cadence as an ops-center or communications-department matter. In an activation, the airport EOC becomes the single point of message coordination for the terminal, and the interfaces multiply fast: TSA (checkpoint/sterile-area messages), CBP (FIS-hall messages), airlines (gate and hold-room paging, some of which the airline controls contractually), ARFF/police (evacuation/shelter-in-place), FBI (in a JOC event), county EM (WEA/IPAWS geo-targeting). A quiet-by-design standard that does not name a single accountable "PA authority having jurisdiction" in the EM plan will produce cross-talk under load.
5. **The FAA regulatory baseline is thin and the AC is prescriptive on planning, silent on acoustics.** 14 CFR § 139.325 requires an AEP with a "communications network" but does not specify intelligibility, zoning, or MNS architecture. AC 150/5200-31C (Change 2) institutionalizes NIMS/ICS and requires coordination with local responders, but leaves acoustic and MNS engineering to the codes (NFPA 72, IBC/IFC as adopted by VA and DC). If MWAA does not set a design standard, no federal regulator will — and each terminal build will re-litigate intelligibility from scratch, badly.
6. **The 2020 stress test and the July 2024 CrowdStrike day rewrote the continuity-of-operations chapter.** COVID made "continuity of operations" a director-level program at every serious hub. The July 19, 2024 CrowdStrike Falcon update grounded 7,000 Delta flights, filled ATL baggage claim aisles wall-to-wall, and cost Delta $550M in stated damages. Both events proved the point: PA and FIDS are dependent systems that fail when their upstream IT stack fails. A quiet-by-design standard must specify what the passenger-communication environment looks like when the primary IT is *degraded*, not just when it is up.
7. **DCA and IAD do not have the same activation profile, and one standard will not fit both.** DCA sits inside the SFRA under permanent elevated federal-security posture, was the site of the January 29, 2025 mid-air collision (67 fatalities, Route 4 closure, Runway 15/33 restrictions), and operates a mixed 1941/Pelli/Project-Journey terminal envelope where retrofit acoustic treatment is architecturally constrained. IAD is a growing 29M-pax international hub with a $20B+ ten-year build now in front of it (announced July 29, 2026). A common MWAA standard should be *tiered*: mandatory intelligibility and emergency-override requirements at both; context-dependent implementation for zoning, quiet rooms, and tenant systems.
8. **The single largest EM risk in this decision is not the standard. It is the succession gap.** Institutional knowledge — who to call at the FBI WFO, at the Virginia Department of Emergency Management, at MWAA Airports Police, at United's Network Operations Center — walks out the door with a senior EM director. A quiet-by-design standard that requires PA authority-having-jurisdiction, EOC override procedures, and multi-agency coordination in an activation is only as good as the person who can convene those partners at 03:15 on a Sunday. That is not a document problem. It is a retention and cross-training problem the standard should force MWAA to solve.

