## 2. Evidence

**Regulatory baseline.** 14 CFR § 139.325 requires each certificated airport to develop and maintain an AEP with procedures for prompt response to aircraft accidents, bomb threats, structural fires, natural disasters, hazmat, sabotage/hijack, water rescue, crowd control, and disabled aircraft removal, and to maintain "a communications network" adequate to implement those procedures [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-G/part-139/subpart-D/section-139.325]. The regulation is silent on acoustics, intelligibility, and MNS engineering — deliberately, because the technical standards live in the codes. FAA AC 150/5200-31C (consolidated with Change 2) provides the format and content guidance for the AEP and institutionalized NIMS/ICS, but again does not set intelligibility thresholds or PA zoning requirements [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5200_31c_consolidated.pdf].

**Intelligibility thresholds.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 (Emergency Communications Systems) requires *both* audibility and intelligibility for voice notification systems, with intelligibility measured by Speech Transmission Index or Common Intelligibility Scale, and a minimum STI of 0.45 (or CIS 0.65) at every occupant location within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space [Source: https://ifmacentralohio.org/images/meeting/101316/nfpa_chapter_24__emergency_communication_systems.pdf; Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/security/mass_notification/mass-notification-intelligibility/]. Systems that meet dBA thresholds but fail STI testing are non-compliant, and jurisdictions have withheld certificates of occupancy on that basis [Source: https://www.suppressionsystems.com/voice-intelligibility-in-mass-notification-systems/].

**Activation evidence — FLL January 6, 2017.** The Police Executive Research Forum's post-incident review of the FLL active-shooter response documented five killed and six wounded, followed by an uncontrolled self-evacuation and an hour-and-a-half of secondary false-shooter panic that emptied concourses onto the AOA [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. The AAR's communications findings — inconsistent messaging across agencies, radio interoperability failures, saturated cellular networks, and no single trusted channel for passenger direction — are the operational reason a quiet-by-design standard has to specify redundant channels, not merely quieter ones.

**Activation evidence — ATL December 17, 2017.** An underground Georgia Power switchgear fire darkened the world's busiest airport for approximately 11 hours, stranding roughly 30,000 passengers, canceling 1,183 flights that Sunday and 400+ the next day, and holding at least 96 aircraft on the tarmac for 3+ hours [Source: https://www.cbsnews.com/news/hartsfield-jackson-atlanta-international-airport-outage-power-back-on-after-chaos-grounded-delta-flights/; Source: https://www.ajc.com/news/atlanta-airport-blog/gao-studied-airport-power-outages-in-wake-of-hartsfield-jackson-blackout/LXTBMWTZ2BEGZLD3VDDSATL6Q4/]. GAO subsequently studied airport power resilience across the system. The event's EM lesson is not "add battery backup"; it is that when the entire electronic passenger-communication stack fails simultaneously, the airports that recovered fastest were the ones with pre-staged staffed communication, portable PA, printed job-aids, and an EOC with authority to run the terminal by voice and paper.

**Activation evidence — CrowdStrike day, July 19, 2024.** A defective Falcon update crashed 8.5M Windows systems worldwide; Delta canceled ~7,000 flights, took ~$550M in stated damages, and turned ATL baggage claim into a "madhouse" of stranded passengers and unclaimed bags [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Delta_Air_Lines_disruption; Source: https://www.marketplace.org/story/2024/07/24/atlanta-airport-delta-crowdstrike-outage; Source: https://www.ciodive.com/news/delta-air-lines-tech-woes-flight-cancelations-crowdstrike-microsoft/722056/]. The relevant EM lesson: PA, FIDS, mobile-app push, and the airline's own communication tools share IT dependencies. The passenger-communication design has to assume a degraded mode.

**Activation evidence — Hawaii false ballistic missile alert, January 13, 2018.** A single operator's misapplication of a WEA/EAS template produced a 38-minute island-wide alert, published across TV, radio, and every cell phone [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Hawaii_false_missile_alert; Source: https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/68/wr/mm6807a2.htm; Source: https://docs.fcc.gov/public/attachments/DOC-350119A1.pdf]. HI-EMA subsequently instituted two-person activation/verification for real and test messages. The EM read-across for MWAA: mass-notification tools are lethal without governance. Any quiet-by-design standard that gives operators an "emergency override" on the PA has to specify authorization, two-person rule, script library, and post-event logging.

**Activation evidence — DCA mid-air collision, January 29, 2025.** PSA 5342 (CRJ-700) and a US Army UH-60 collided over the Potomac on approach to Runway 33; 67 fatalities. NTSB (January 2026 board meeting) issued 50 recommendations to FAA, US Army, DoD, and DOT; FAA closed Route 4 and published an Interim Final Rule restricting Runways 15/33 during helicopter operations [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/Pages/DCA25MA108.aspx; Source: https://www.faa.gov/newsroom/faa-statements-midair-collision-reagan-washington-national-airport; Source: https://enotrans.org/article/ntsb-unveils-findings-probable-cause-and-recommendations-from-dca-mid-air-collision-investigation/]. For the DCA terminal environment, the event confirmed that mass-fatality response — family assistance center standup, media staging, mass-notification to affected families — remains the highest-consequence EOC use case, and one where PA intelligibility, quiet rooms for family reception, and press-holding zoning matter more than daily-operations acoustics.

**Doctrinal evidence — airport EM as an integrated director-level program.** ACRP Research Report 189, *Design Considerations for Airport EOCs*, treats physical space, technology, function, operations, and governance as one integrated design problem and explicitly frames the EOC as a design object, not a room [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrp0715/wp-content/themes/acrp-child/documents/214/original/acrp_r189.pdf; Source: https://www.nationalacademies.org/publications/25280]. ACRP Synthesis 115, *Practices in Airport Emergency Plans*, surveys peer AEPs (including FLL, CLT, IAH, SEA, LAS) and documents the range of activation-triggering criteria, position-based versus person-based command, and the observed gap between the activation matrix and the actual call tree [Source: https://www.tpm-portal.com/document/practices-in-airport-emergency-plans-acrp-synthesis-report-115/]. ACRP Research Report 93 established that operational and business continuity is a distinct planning discipline for prolonged airport disruptions [Source: https://onlinepubs.trb.org/onlinepubs/acrp/docs/CompilationReport.pdf].

**Doctrinal evidence — terminal activation.** ACRP Synthesis 20, *Airport Terminal Facility Activation Techniques*, catalogues lessons from 13 domestic and international terminal openings and identifies commissioning of communication systems (PA, FIDS, MNS) as a recurring source of Day-1 failure when not verified against measured performance criteria pre-open [Source: https://crp.trb.org/acrpwebresource2/acrp-synthesis-20-airport-terminal-facility-activation-techniques/; Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/14394/chapter/8]. The direct implication for MWAA: any quiet-by-design standard has to require STI/CIS field verification before beneficial occupancy of IAD Concourse E (opening September 2026) and every subsequent phase, not after complaints.

