## 3. The preparedness case — for and against the thesis

**For the thesis (an EM director's case *for* a common quiet-by-design standard).**

- A standard that specifies STI ≥ 0.50 in every ADS, NC/RC background-noise ceilings, RT60 targets appropriate to terminal geometry, and mandatory zoning of the PA down to the gate hold-room level is the most efficient way to close the intelligibility gap that FLL and every large-hub PA survey exposes. Intelligible emergency PA is a *life-safety* attribute; if the standard makes it non-negotiable at design, MWAA never has to re-fight it in commissioning, contract change orders, or after an event.
- A standard that requires an EOC-controlled emergency override on every zone, dual-path visual notification (FIDS + digital signage + gate displays), IPAWS/WEA geo-fence capability, and a documented staffed-communication concept turns "quiet" into a *resilience* posture. When the primary PA fails (ATL 2017, any CrowdStrike-class IT event), the redundant layers carry the load.
- A standard that names PA authority-having-jurisdiction in an activation — EOC Manager during activation, ops-center supervisor otherwise, with delegated scripts — eliminates the cross-talk failure mode. It also gives the FSD, CBP port director, ARFF chief, and airline duty managers a single point of coordination for terminal-wide messaging.
- The economics are one-directional. Every acoustic and MNS attribute is 3–10x cheaper baked into program brief and design than added in commissioning or retrofit. The July 29, 2026 Dulles announcement — $20B+ over ten years — is the largest window MWAA will have this generation to install the standard before designs harden.

**Against the thesis (what a peer EM director would push back on).**

- If "quiet-by-design" is written by architects and customer-experience consultants without EM at the table, the standard will optimize for daily ambient dB and inadvertently *cap* the emergency-override output required by NFPA 72. That is a life-safety regression disguised as passenger experience.
- If the standard treats reduced announcement frequency as a goal in itself, it can normalize a culture in which line operators are reluctant to make required security or safety announcements. FLL 2017's uncontrolled self-evacuation started, in part, because there was no authoritative airport voice competing with rumor. A standard that celebrates announcement-frequency reduction as a metric will make that failure mode more likely, not less.
- Common standards across DCA and IAD are appealing on paper but will collide with the architectural constraints of DCA Terminal 1 (1941 Bolling, historic-district review), the Pelli-era Terminal 2, and the SFRA operating posture. A mandatory acoustic target that cannot be met inside a protected historic envelope creates a permanent variance regime — which becomes a permanent audit finding.
- Governance risk: If MWAA adopts a standard but does not staff and cross-train the EM function to sustain the announcement-authority and emergency-override discipline the standard requires, the standard is inert. Institutional knowledge is the load-bearing wall. A 25-year EM director cannot be replaced from outside hiring in 90 days; the programs that survived a transition did it by deliberate 3–5-year deputy-development pipelines, documented relationship maps, and quarterly full-scale exercises that force the incoming leader into the muscle memory. The standard should require both.

**On balance from this chair:** a properly written quiet-by-design standard is one of the highest-return investments MWAA can make in emergency-communication resilience over the next decade. A poorly written one is a life-safety liability with a customer-experience brochure attached. The Council should recommend adoption, on condition that EM is a signatory author and that the standard's compliance regime is enforced through commissioning and periodic re-verification, not aspirational language.

