## 4. Program anchors — 3–5 events, exercises, and program details a strategist can use

1. **FLL active-shooter incident, January 6, 2017** — the definitive US airport case study on multi-channel passenger communication failure under duress and the cost of not having a single trusted airport voice. PERF AAR is the primary document. Anchor for the "quiet ≠ silent" argument.
2. **ATL power outage, December 17, 2017** — the definitive US airport case on total electronic-communication failure and the value of pre-staged staffed and analog channels. GAO follow-on and industry-wide power-resilience shift are the corrective-action record. Anchor for degraded-mode design.
3. **CrowdStrike Falcon incident, July 19, 2024** — the definitive recent case on shared-IT-dependency failure across airline, airport, and MNS stacks. Delta $550M loss, ATL "madhouse," and DOT investigation are the public record. Anchor for the requirement that a quiet-by-design standard specify degraded-mode passenger communication.
4. **DCA mid-air collision, January 29, 2025** — the highest-consequence recent event on the MWAA footprint. NTSB January 2026 findings, Route 4 closure, and Runway 15/33 IFR are the corrective-action record. Anchor for family-assistance, media-staging, and mass-notification design requirements specifically at DCA.
5. **ACRP Research Report 189 (Design Considerations for Airport EOCs) + ACRP Synthesis 20 (Terminal Facility Activation Techniques) + ACRP Synthesis 115 (Practices in Airport Emergency Plans)** — the doctrinal triangulation any credible MWAA standard should cite. Anchor for the "this is not a novel idea; the industry has done the research; MWAA is late" argument.

**One-line thesis for the strategist:** *Quiet-by-design is emergency-management infrastructure. Write the standard as if the EOC will use it at 03:15 on a Sunday during a mass-casualty activation, because eventually it will.*
